{# The Huntsville Problem #}
<section class="content-section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <p class="section-eyebrow">The Huntsville Problem</p>
      <h2>Why Your Current Gutters Are Failing</h2>
    </div>
    
    <div class="content-grid">
      <div class="content-text">
        <p>Let's be honest about what's happening to gutters across Huntsville: they're failing faster than they should. Drive through Hampton Cove, Big Cove, or Jones Valley and you'll see it — gutters pulling away from fascia boards, water pouring over edges during rain, rust stains running down vinyl siding.</p>
        
        <p>This isn't bad luck. It's predictable. Those subdivisions that went up during Huntsville's building boom (roughly 2000-2015) all share the same problem: builder-grade gutters. That means 5-inch gutters when 6-inch was needed, .027 gauge aluminum that dents when you lean a ladder on it, and hangers spaced 32 inches apart — the minimum that meets code.</p>
        
        <p>Those systems weren't built for North Alabama. They were built to pass inspection and keep costs low. Now, 10-15 years later, the seams are leaking, the hangers are failing, and homeowners are dealing with the consequences.</p>
        
        <h3>The Triple Threat: Rain, Clay, and Humidity</h3>
        
        <p>Huntsville throws three challenges at every gutter system:</p>
        
        <p><strong>Annual Rainfall:</strong> We get 55+ inches per year — more than Seattle, Portland, or Denver. And unlike the Pacific Northwest, our rain comes in violent bursts. A spring thunderstorm can dump 2-3 inches in an hour. Five-inch gutters can't keep up. They overflow. Water pours over the front edge, saturating the ground right next to your foundation.</p>
        
        <p><strong>Red Clay Soil:</strong> Madison County sits on clay that simply doesn't absorb water. When your gutters overflow, that water doesn't soak into the ground — it pools. It creeps toward your foundation. It finds the lowest point and sits there. Over years, this creates the foundation cracks, basement moisture, and crawl space problems that plague so many Huntsville homes.</p>
        
        <p><strong>Humidity Damage:</strong> Alabama summers are brutal on wood. Fascia boards, exposed to humidity and trapped moisture from failing gutters, rot faster than homeowners expect. By the time gutters are sagging and pulling away, the fascia behind them needs replacement too. We see this constantly — especially in established neighborhoods like Five Points, Twickenham, and Blossomwood where homes are 30-50 years old.</p>
      </div>
      
