{# What Are Seamless Gutters #}
<section class="content-section alt-bg">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <p class="section-eyebrow">The Smart Choice</p>
      <h2>What Are Seamless Gutters?</h2>
    </div>
    
    <div class="content-text-full">
      <p>Seamless gutters are fabricated from a single piece of aluminum coil — rolled on-site by a portable machine we bring to your driveway. Instead of 10-foot sections joined with seams every 10 feet, you get continuous runs with seams only at corners and downspout connections.</p>
      
      <p>Why does this matter? Every seam in a gutter system is a future failure point. Sealant degrades. Joints flex and separate. Debris catches at the overlap. A 50-foot run of sectional gutters has five potential leak points. Seamless eliminates all five.</p>
      
      <h3>What We Actually Install</h3>
      
      <p>When Blue River Gutters installs seamless gutters in Huntsville, here's exactly what you get:</p>
      
      <ul class="specs-list">
        <li><strong>6-inch K-style profile:</strong> The industry standard for residential homes. K-style handles more water than half-round and the flat back mounts flush against fascia boards.</li>
        <li><strong>.032 gauge aluminum:</strong> About 20% thicker than the .027 gauge most companies use. Doesn't dent when you lean a ladder on it. Resists hail damage better. Lasts longer.</li>
        <li><strong>Hidden hangers every 24 inches:</strong> Not the 32-inch spacing that meets minimum code. Closer spacing means less sagging, especially important when ice loads up in winter or debris accumulates in fall.</li>
        <li><strong>3x4 downspouts:</strong> Larger than the 2x3 downspouts that come standard with builder-grade systems. More capacity = less overflow during heavy rain.</li>
        <li><strong>One downspout every 35-40 linear feet:</strong> That's the math for Huntsville's rainfall volume. Fewer than this and you're asking for overflow.</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h3>The Honest Part About "Seamless"</h3>
      
      <p>We should be upfront: "seamless" is a slight exaggeration. You still have seams at inside corners, outside corners, and downspout outlets. What you don't have is mid-run seams — and that's what actually fails. The corner seams are properly sealed and rarely cause problems. It's the seams every 10 feet in sectional gutters that degrade and leak.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

